      "text": "To determine the value of \\(a\\) such that the two given lines are perpendicular, we need to find the slopes of the lines and use the condition that the product of the slopes of two perpendicular lines is \\(-1\\).\n\nThe first line is already in slope-intercept form \\(y = mx + b\\), so its slope is the coefficient of \\(x\\). This gives the slope \\(m_1 = 2\\).\n\nFor the second line, we first rewrite it in slope-intercept form:\n\\[\n6y + ax = 6.\n\\]\nWe then isolate \\(y\\):\n\\[\n6y = -ax + 6 \\implies y = -\\frac{a}{6}x + 1.\n\\]\nThus, the slope of the second line is \\(-\\frac{a}{6}\\).\n\nThe condition for two lines to be perpendicular is that the product of their slopes is \\(-1\\). So, we set up the equation:\n\\[\n2 \\times \\left(-\\frac{a}{6}\\right) = -1.\n\\]\nNow, solve this equation for \\(a\\):\n\\[\n-\\frac{2a}{6} = -1 \\implies \\frac{2a}{6} = 1 \\implies \\frac{a}{3} = 1 \\implies a = 3.\n\\]\nThus, the value of \\(a\\) is \\(3\\).\n\nLet's confirm this with Python code.\n```python\nimport sympy as sp\n\n# Define the variable\na = sp.symbols('a')\n\n# Slopes of the lines\nslope1 = 2\nslope2 = -a/6\n\n# Condition for perpendicularity\nperpendicular_condition = sp.Eq(slope1 * slope2, -1)\n\n# Solve for a\nsolution = sp.solve(perpendicular_condition, a)\nprint(solution)\n```\n```output\n[3]\n```\nThe value of \\(a\\) that makes the two given lines perpendicular is indeed \\(\\boxed{3}\\).",
